  $tsh$[{"issue": "slack_sdk.errors.SlackApiError: not_in_channel", "fix": "Invite the bot to that channel first: type `/invite @YourBotName` in Slack. The bot can only read the history of channels it's actually a member of."}, {"issue": "slack_sdk.errors.SlackApiError: missing_scope", "fix": "Go back to OAuth & Permissions in your Slack app settings, add the missing scope under Bot Token Scopes, then click Reinstall to Workspace — adding a scope doesn't take effect until you reinstall."}, {"issue": "The briefing arrives but both sections just say “Nothing to report”", "fix": "Check that your channels actually had messages in the last 24 hours, and that CHANNEL_IDS in main.py has the right IDs (right-click a channel name → View channel details, scroll to the bottom to find the ID starting with C)."}, {"issue": "A scheduled run logs “The AI replied, but not in the TOP/FYI format we asked for” and no DM arrives", "fix": "This is summarizer.py's parse_briefing() catching a malformed AI reply on purpose — sending nothing beats sending you a half-garbled DM at 8am. It's usually a one-off; the next scheduled run tries again on its own. If it keeps happening, add `print(reply)` inside summarize_briefing() to see exactly what the AI sent back."}, {"issue": "python main.py prints a message and exits immediately without trying Slack at all", "fix": "That's intentional — main.py checks SLACK_BOT_TOKEN, SLACK_USER_ID, and CHANNEL_IDS before making any network calls, and tells you exactly which one is missing instead of failing partway through."}, {"issue": "The cron job (or Task Scheduler task) never seems to run", "fix": "Cron and Task Scheduler both need the absolute path to your virtual environment's Python, not just `python`. Run the exact command from your scheduled entry by hand in a terminal first to confirm it works before trusting it to the schedule."}]$tsh$::jsonb,
